00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:08,080
So to kick things off, I always ask people three questions.

30
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:13,360
So this is before we get into like Fantastical and Flexibits, I want to introduce people to who you are.

31
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,320
And so the three questions I always ask are, where are you from?

32
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:18,800
Do you have a formal education related to what you do?

33
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,400
And then what was your career like leading up to Flexibits?

34
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:23,360
Great questions.

35
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,960
And I'm going to talk a lot now, because there's a lot to that.

36
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:34,560
Yep, that's why I just barrage you with three questions, and then I usually step back and try to close my mouth, because this is where the good stuff comes.

37
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:35,920
Yeah, so good questions.

38
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,680
So the first thing is, I'm from New York.

39
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:47,600
I grew up on Long Island, New York, if anyone is familiar with it, actually a town called Massapequa, which most people seem to know it whenever I mention it, but yeah, I grew up in New York.

40
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:54,160
And the second question was, do I have any formal background in what I do?

41
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:01,920
Not formal in the thinking that, you know, I went to college for computer science or specific something towards it.

42
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:09,360
But what's interesting is I did go to college for communications and media arts, so like video film broadcasting and movies.

43
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:09,840
Yeah.

44
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:13,520
And I personally find that very related.

45
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:15,920
I personally find that very intertwined.

46
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,720
At the time when I was going to school, I probably didn't think of it that way.

47
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:29,200
But to me, technology, software, whether it's games, even productivity, which I view it differently, I think an experience matters, right?

48
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:35,920
I think the experience you have with the software you use, even if it's a quote unquote boring tool, should be an experience.

49
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:44,480
And a lot of what I went to school for, media, communication, you know, performance, if you will, I like to have that kind of show in whatever I build.

50
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:45,040
Yeah.

51
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:48,240
I mean, I obviously, very much agree with that.

52
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:59,760
That is, I didn't actually have a formal background in that, but that was like my, that's always been my side hustle until I got into the Iowa space was animation and video production and stuff.

53
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:01,520
And it's kind of a similar deal.

54
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:18,320
It's like the most boring software can still be a, you know, you create these experiences for people to get them into viewing data however they want or whatever, but you can like construct a narrative and inject delight in there in the same way as you can do a production or something.

55
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:20,720
You're still trying to make people feel a certain way.

56
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:21,440
Absolutely.

57
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:26,960
I think of all my projects whenever I do them, even a feature, as sort of a movie and how would I present it.

58
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,320
Like, interesting.

59
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:41,680
The other interesting part is when I started out, like when I was young, these were the Commodore 64, Vic 20, eventually Amiga days, and there was no way to really learn a computer per se.

60
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,760
You kind of bought one and self taught yourself.

61
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:47,680
And that computer affinity I had growing up, right?

62
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:55,600
Really just immersing myself into all that stuff, eventually which then led into my movie film communication days.

63
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,640
I really feel like that was the harvest of, yeah, I know how to use a computer and I really love computers.

64
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:02,960
Yeah, I really love good experiences.

65
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:03,920
Let's marry them.

66
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:06,240
And that's kind of the direction my career went off into.

67
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:12,160
Would you say that you were at the intersection of technology and liberal arts?

68
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:14,080
I would one hundred percent say I was.

69
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:18,640
Because actually, believe it or not, the college I went to was very into liberal arts.

70
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:20,560
And it's one of the reasons I chose it.

71
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:24,640
And actually the foundation besides film and communication, a lot of the stuff that we studied there.

72
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:28,880
There was actually a course that was mandatory, which was humanities.

73
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:37,200
And I always say because of that, because of that liberal arts, that humanities that really allows, really a lot of empathy in software design, right?

74
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:38,480
Because you really think about people.

75
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,320
And that's who's using software, right?

76
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,960
That's who I want to ultimately benefit from what I create.

77
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:49,280
So I'm guessing based on that background then, and this is a total guess, I don't actually know the answer here.

78
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:56,640
The Mac in that whole, I mean, that phrase, you know, that I was making the joke about, just was right in your wheelhouse?

79
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:59,920
Did you jump on that like right away and get really excited about it?

80
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:00,960
Not at all.

81
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:01,840
Oh, okay.

82
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:11,360
You'll be Yeah, a lot of people are surprised to hear this, but I wasn't really a Mac user until college, until around roughly my junior year.

83
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:19,840
So, um, before that I was a hardcore, hardcore Amiga user and a hardcore, hardcore Commodore 64 user.

84
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:30,480
Um, obviously had a lot of video game systems, you know, my child of the eighty, so Atari 2600 and television, Vectrex and on and on and on, um, Sega Genesis eventually Nintendo, blah, blah, blah.

85
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:33,200
But my point was, no, I wasn't into the Mac at all.

86
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:37,440
Um, I got into it in college, actually, thanks to a roommate of mine mine who had one.

87
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,240
And I remember at that time the Amiga was kind of going away, was just dying out.

88
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:46,880
And I remember seeing his Mac and he was very into customizing it.

89
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,600
Like he would customize his hard drive icon and all these other things.

90
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,360
And I remember saying, Oh, that's really neat.

91
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:52,960
Like there's these things on Mac.

92
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:56,480
I never knew Mac could be this kind of personal, right?

93
00:06:56,960 --> 00:06:57,120
Right.

94
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,120
And it just, you know, it attracted me.

95
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:01,680
And then eventually I got a Mac and the rest is history.

96
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:02,080
Okay.

97
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:06,800
So the rest is history is, it could be the other title of the show at this point.

98
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,200
So we're going to keep digging into that.

99
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:17,520
So, you graduated college then., you hadn't like transitioned into a technology role at that point, or degree, I guess?

100
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:18,080
No.

101
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:23,520
So by that point, and just to be clear, you're talking about where I was getting, starting to get into the Mac?

102
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:28,080
Well, I'm just saying, when you graduated, where did you go from there?

103
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:30,960
Did you jump into technology right away or was there some sort of a detour?

104
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:34,960
So I actually did jump into technology, but not in the way you think.

105
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:37,920
I actually started working for a video game company.

106
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:52,000
So there's technology, yeah, there's technology for certain, but it was more I always view this is very strange, and I know that people agree with me or probably get angry at me and send me hate mail, but I see video games as very different from computing or computers.

107
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:56,080
Maybe it's for when I grew up, maybe I'm right, maybe I'm wrong, I don't know.

108
00:07:56,240 --> 00:08:00,320
But like video games for me are more entertainment and more very creative.

109
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,160
They're like art forms, like movies.

110
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:08,480
And apps like Fantastical or Card Hop or whatever that are apps, I'm not saying they can't be creative, right?

111
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:12,720
Or get into where I see an experience being friendly and fun and humanistic.

112
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,160
But video games for me just feel like a different industry.

113
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:17,280
I think they always will.

114
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:23,040
Again, it might be for my generation or when I grew up, but I see video games as something very different but very similar.

115
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:23,360
No.

116
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,600
I mean, unless you're James Thompson, in which case you can do both at the same time.

117
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:32,000
I think they're definitely like parallel universes to a degree.

118
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:44,480
Although I do think that both can and do frequently learn from each other and taking ideas from either, there's a lot of I mean, the same goes for film or music or anything, I guess.

119
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:50,080
But I definitely feel like they're on very different tracks as far as goals.

120
00:08:50,400 --> 00:09:00,160
They might both technically be running through a processor changing ones to zeros, but they're very different in terms of how you would construct them.

121
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:03,600
and how are they certainly how they're consumed?

122
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:04,640
Oh, they definitely are.

123
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:06,640
And actually, a good way of putting it is this way.

124
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:12,720
I would never, when I graduated working for that video game company, ever thought I'd be making software for a living.

125
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:16,480
You know, I thought I'd be in maybe the gaming industry or doing video games, whatever it is.

126
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:27,440
And that was my mindset at that time that I definitely saw apps, you know, at that point was like, you know, applications such as maybe a word processor or a graphic app, right?

127
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:31,520
In those days, and we're talking about like 95, 96, I'm trying to remember exactly what it was.

128
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,040
Lotus Notes or whatever.

129
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:35,680
Yeah, you didn't really have that many applications.

130
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:41,600
I'm certain if we went back to that time frame and saw, like, for a computer period, how many applications were there?

131
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:44,160
Maybe a few thousand, but like, you know what I mean?

132
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:45,920
Really nothing great, big.

133
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:47,760
You know, it's only a few big names.

134
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:54,800
So yeah, but yeah, definitely see it differently as, you know, the video game industry was where I thought I was going in and that was my first gig.

135
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:58,720
Okay, so were you like programming at that time, designing?

136
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:00,480
What was your actual role?

137
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:03,760
No, so my role was actually initially QA.

138
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:18,480
I was an analyst, a QA analyst eventually, and what that meant was not only finding bugs, not only trying to find like continuity issues and errors and problems with the code and the games, but also coming up with ideas for the games and analyzing other games.

139
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:27,120
So we had gotten some games that were imported from other countries, and I got to give input on what I thought it was, how the continuity was, if it would work in America.

140
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:30,800
It was more of a QA slash analyst role, if you will.

141
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:31,840
Interesting, okay.

142
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:33,600
And it's really neat.

143
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:34,720
I really like that job a lot.

144
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:35,440
It was a lot of fun.

145
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:51,120
Yeah, I mean, it sounds, especially at that time when, I mean, I guess the same was for software, but like so much of video games was defining itself at that time in terms of what it was as an industry, as a user experience., all those things, that would have been wild.

146
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:52,240
Yeah, there were fun times.

147
00:10:52,480 --> 00:11:00,880
I mean, that was just when the PlayStation was coming out and just when the Sega Saturn was coming out, you know, 32 bit gaming and 64 bit gaming and really just starting to push the envelopes.

148
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:04,960
And, you know, fighter games were getting more polygons and everything.

149
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:06,240
It was a fun time.

150
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:10,400
I mean, I just remember seeing some of the stuff being like, man, we're really accelerating now.

151
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:14,400
So how did you transition from that to software?

152
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:20,640
So one of the questions I think you were asking, which I want to just touch on a little bit, is I don't have any programming background formally.

153
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:27,920
I did dabble in programming on the Commodore 64 in my Amiga days in what I would call crude programming of hacking.

154
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,640
So I knew enough to get into code back then.

155
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:35,040
It was different because it was assembly and a lot of hacking and cracking, if you will.

156
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:36,880
Well, it was cracking.

157
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:40,480
But the point is, it wasn't writing code to make something work.

158
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:42,800
It was sort of taking code and making things work.

159
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:47,360
I don't want to say cut and paste coding because it just wasn't that way, but similar to that.

160
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,840
So I always had an interest in code.

161
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:51,760
I always understood what the code did.

162
00:11:52,240 --> 00:11:55,680
I always, you know, basically know how this stuff works.

163
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:00,720
But I never really directly had this I'm going to go into that and build stuff.

164
00:12:01,520 --> 00:12:03,280
I wanted to build stuff with other people.

165
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:11,360
So like I knew a lot of coders or a lot of at that time engineers, programmers, that I'd have all these ideas and be like, hey, let's do this.

166
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:12,400
And they'd be like, that's great.

167
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:13,600
We don't have any good ideas.

168
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:24,000
So instead of them sitting around doing nothing, I was kind of the idea guy, but I knew enough about the code to not just say, hey, let's build this thing that will reverse time and they're like, we can't do that.

169
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:34,560
So it was always very nice that I had the knowledge of what could be done and sort of where the boundaries are, but then, you know, work with someone that could get it done and complement them.

170
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:35,040
Okay.

171
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:39,920
So, and that was that was my that was even in the game at the game company you were at?

172
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:41,360
Yeah, that was in the game company.

173
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:42,480
And then that starts fast forward.

174
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:44,880
So to ask you, you know, you're then asked, how did I transition?

175
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:57,920
So after the game company, I ended up working for one of the biggest, well, it was really the biggest in New York, but telecom companies that did cable TV and a lot of entertainment, DVR.

176
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:00,000
Like I worked in the new media group.

177
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:04,400
So I was literally on the front of DVR and Tivo and all these things that we have today.

178
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:06,320
We're just taking it for granted.

179
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:07,840
Streaming, right, exactly.

180
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:08,880
So that was a lot of fun.

181
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:13,440
And that I was a project manager, product manager, and I actually did a lot of user interface stuff there.

182
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:18,400
So that transition from video games to more, if you will, apps, right?

183
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:21,920
Because these were apps on the front end controlling the streaming for the video.

184
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:30,640
User interface is how you actually make things start, how you do purchases, how things appear, how buttons appear, how people interpret the user interface.

185
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:34,320
The actual experience of a human being interacting with that software.

186
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:35,040
Exactly.

187
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:36,240
And that's what I knew.

188
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:40,640
I loved user experience, user interface, the interaction part of it.

189
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:42,240
I realized that was my forte.

190
00:13:42,960 --> 00:13:49,680
Just like with the film, just like with the movies, I liked making sure that everything was pleasant, eas easy and simple.

191
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:52,880
That was my thing, that was my passion.

192
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:14,720
There's been a lot of jobs in between there and flexibits, but the short version is I got, ultimately, back into the Mac industry or got into the Mac industry right after CableVision, there was a company in California that wanted me to become the head of product for these software drivers they were making.

193
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:19,600
It was like hard disk drivers and CD run burning drivers, CDR drivers.

194
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:23,360
That got me into the Mac industry.

195
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:29,120
And from there it's been a wild ride and obviously to get into my own gig at Flexibits.

196
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:37,520
But yeah, that was the real, okay, you're getting into software, moving out to California, working for a company that makes Apple software.

197
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:40,080
And then, yeah, a lot of things after that.

198
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:42,000
I'm happy to go into them, but there's a lot.

199
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:48,160
Oh yeah, I mean, that's what this show really is, is like, I kind of want to get into where Flexibits came from?

200
00:14:48,560 --> 00:14:48,960
Yeah, sure.

201
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:52,320
So, yeah, so that company was called ProSoft.

202
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:54,960
They actually had a subsidiary called RadioLogic.

203
00:14:55,280 --> 00:15:00,320
RadioLogic was the company that did the CD drivers, hard disk drivers, a lot of utility type stuff.

204
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:03,600
Very esoteric today, no one would really care.

205
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:17,440
